<div id="serial-panel" style="display: none;">
  <div class="oscar-serial">
    <p class="oscar-serial-hint">
      For an Arduino, or any board, on a USB cable plugged into the computer
      running OSCAR. Connect its port here, then set a widget's <b>Ip</b> to
      <code>serial</code>. The widget's Port is not used. Messages go out as
      OSC framed with SLIP.
    </p>
    <p class="oscar-serial-hint">
      A board on Wi-Fi or Ethernet needs none of this: give the widget the
      board's IP address and port, as for any other software. Sketches for
      both are in <code>tools/arduino</code>.
    </p>

    <div id="serial-status" class="oscar-serial-status" role="status">Checking...</div>

    <div class="oscar-serial-controls">
      <select id="serial-port" class="o-input" aria-label="Serial port"></select>
      <select
        id="serial-bitrate"
        class="o-input oscar-serial-bitrate"
        aria-label="Baud rate"
        title="Baud rate: the number in Serial.begin() in your sketch"
      ></select>
    </div>

    <div class="oscar-serial-actions">
      <button id="serial-refresh" class="o-btn" type="button">Refresh list</button>
      <button id="serial-connect" class="o-btn o-btn-primary" type="button">Connect</button>
      <button id="serial-disconnect" class="o-btn" type="button">Disconnect</button>
    </div>

    <p class="oscar-serial-note">
      Only one program can hold a serial port. Close the Arduino IDE's Serial
      Monitor before connecting, and disconnect here before uploading a sketch.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
